WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:09.570
The 1995 movie The Net is a paranoid thriller
about this oddity called the internet.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:14.599
“Our whole world is sitting there on a
computer, it’s in the computer.”

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:20.270
Sandra Bullock’s character chats with Cyberbob,
orders her pizza off Pizza.net,

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:24.500
and hangs out with Dennis Miller during that
brief window when he was a feature film actor.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.840
“This is, this is bizarre.”
As an early cyber thriller, The Net introduced

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:34.250
the internet and, as importantly, a
“look” for the internet to a lot of people.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:39.640
But those webpages weren’t real. Someone
had to get those invented graphics on Sandra

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:40.660
Bullock’s computer screen.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.410
“My name’s Todd Marks, I’m a computer
video playback

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.860
supervisor for feature film, television, and
commercials.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.030
I’ve been doing this for about 25 years
now.”

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:55.000
Todd’s work shows the incredible detail
that goes into major motion pictures, and

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.410
reveals secrets behind the screens within
screens in movies from

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.390
The Net to
Steve Jobs.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:10.170
But it also uncovers a key to how big stories
are told well.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:20.860
A computer playback supervisor gets the
graphics on the screens in TV shows or movies.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.470
Sometimes the screens you see in movies are
added after the fact, and sometimes they’re

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:28.780
prepared beforehand to shoot along with the
scene. People like Todd have to figure out

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.340
how to display and make those graphics. Well,
as Todd explains:

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:39.810
“Well, the challenges of doing any production
graphics, motion graphics, or playback

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:45.950
graphics for a film is to do something that’s
visually interesting, conveys the message,

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:51.660
and is plausible — those are my three big
things.”

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:57.200
In the comedy Anchorman II, challenges common
to Todd’s job are especially clear.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.270
The comedy’s set in the early 1980s at the
dawn of cable.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:05.869
“We’re starting a 24 hour news channel.
GNN, the global news network.”

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:12.950
“That is without a doubt the dumbest thing
I’ve ever heard.”

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:17.280
That meant there were tons of old TVs in the
background — each of which had to be period

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.739
appropriate physically and display something
period appropriate, too.

00:02:21.739 --> 00:02:25.640
They filmed a lot of the footage on their
own, because the easiest way to get something

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.570
that looked like it was from the 80s was to
make it.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:33.340
“OK, what’s gonna go on this screen, what’s
gonna go on that screen, and what’s happening...

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:38.230
Will Ferrell is coming into the studio, so
we’re not on air yet. What’s on the screens

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.450
then?”
In this case, Todd had to plan — a lot.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:46.590
This chart is typical of how a playback supervisor
would need to plan for a busy shoot. It breaks

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:51.230
down the necessary shots.
Ron smokes crack on air? There’s a neatly

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:55.379
ordered cell in the spreadsheet to get everything
necessary for the shot.

00:02:55.379 --> 00:02:59.900
Other documents carry more specifics. When
Ron and the team meet their new co-worker,

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.129
Linda Jackson,
Todd had a list of exactly what background

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:08.170
elements were necessary, like the three wall
TVs.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:12.510
That planning allows big gags to work without
a hitch.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:16.970
When idiot weatherman Brick, played by Steve
Carrell, went in front of a green screen in

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:20.980
green pants, it was clear the scene would
work better if Todd and his team made the

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.780
effect happen live.
“It wasn’t that tricky. But the production

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:29.190
hadn’t really planned on doing it live,
and we were like, no, we’re doing this live,

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.950
and they were like, ‘Oh, great.’”
The result of all that is

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:39.250
“AAAHHHHH.”
In Team America, planning meant finding the

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:42.909
perfect tiny LCD screens for Kim Jong Il’s
palace.

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:50.260
“The smaller scale there’s little 7 inch
screens right there, 7.5 inch screens, and

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.879
you can see me, I had to like
lean in without falling into the set.”

00:03:54.879 --> 00:04:00.200
In the comedy, The Internship, which was set
at Google, it meant finding the right giant

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:04.810
screens for corporate presentations.
But these screens aren’t only important

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:11.560
details. Sometimes, they can be a key turning
point for a story.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:17.519
“With Date Night, in that particular scene
with Mark Wahlberg, he was

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:22.229
a high tech security expert.”
Now you might not expect Date Night to include

00:04:22.229 --> 00:04:26.589
a technically driven moment. It’’s a very
light 2010 comedy.

00:04:26.589 --> 00:04:31.220
But Todd’s work in this scene is crucial
to landing one of the movie’s best jokes.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:42.849
In it, Mark Wahlberg parodies a trope of over-the-top
action movie computer stuff. It ends up being

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:46.110
pretty funny — and that joke required a
lot of work.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:51.639
“Sp then we have to create a whole logic
to the thing and kind of map out what we want

00:04:51.639 --> 00:04:56.319
to do.”
One silly gag required elaborate mockups,

00:04:56.319 --> 00:05:01.449
rejected concepts,
and designs to perfectly sync the digitally

00:05:01.449 --> 00:05:06.180
inserted screen
with the practical side units on the wall.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:11.119
You might not be looking for a coherent user
interface design in Date Night. But see how

00:05:11.119 --> 00:05:16.360
they bothered with the color of that circular
control? The filmmakers cared because it was

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:20.860
key to making the joke work.
That attention to design was important in

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:24.339
a different way in the period piece Steve
Jobs,

00:05:24.339 --> 00:05:30.020
which was about design, computers, and presentation
— basically everything someone like Todd

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:33.330
cares about.
“As far as the challenges, there were so

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:36.780
many challenges
involved in that project.”

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.870
Challenges like making computers from 1984
look new again.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:46.789
“You could put the old Macs apart and put
this paste on them, and leave them out in

00:05:46.789 --> 00:05:51.509
the sun and bleach the plastics.”
...Or finding the perfect build of blueberry

00:05:51.509 --> 00:05:57.089
iMac. These details were key to making the
movie — and the movie’s emotions — feel

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:59.939
real.
One of the most touching scenes in the movie

00:05:59.939 --> 00:06:04.710
is when Steve Jobs connects with his daughter
over a new computer. The character is normally

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:10.179
distant, but he’s able to be more open and
warm because of software. And in the movie,

00:06:10.179 --> 00:06:14.689
they didn’t fake it.
“That was a Macintosh, and that was a special

00:06:14.689 --> 00:06:20.139
one. John, our engineer, had actually built
a special video card for that one.”

00:06:20.139 --> 00:06:25.240
“Push that key and the S at the same time.”
“And we had to load everything off of floppies.”

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:26.779
“Now type your name.”

00:06:26.779 --> 00:06:31.969
“The artwork was created by our artist in
Photoshop, so now we had to figure out how

00:06:31.969 --> 00:06:37.020
to get this Photoshop file back down to a
400k floppy.”

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.969
“Do you know which box is save?”

00:06:39.969 --> 00:06:48.340
“it was really booting off the real Macintosh
software. You know, it’s craziness!”

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:53.811
“If there’s something that the actor can
act against, it’s much better...it’s better

00:06:53.811 --> 00:07:00.009
for the director, it’s better for timing
when things are cued and when people can respond.

00:07:00.009 --> 00:07:04.889
It’s better for the editors, because they
have something to edit against, versus trying

00:07:04.889 --> 00:07:09.789
to cut to something that’s not there. It
just makes the whole process smoother, even

00:07:09.789 --> 00:07:13.879
if there’s something that needs to be changed
later and they have to burn it in later, which

00:07:13.879 --> 00:07:15.249
sometimes happens.”

00:07:15.249 --> 00:07:20.439
That makes it worth the difficulty and the
stress, worth doing live instead of after

00:07:20.439 --> 00:07:22.089
the fact.
The gimmick in The Net was

00:07:22.089 --> 00:07:25.539
a hidden backdoor that basically unlocks the
internet.

00:07:25.539 --> 00:07:29.479
“You click on the little Pi symbol while
you’re holding this key, and then you get

00:07:29.479 --> 00:07:35.110
into that, which again was pretty ridiculous
— at least it moved the story forward and

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:39.919
didn’t pull you out.”
The idea’s implausible, but kind of compelling:

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:45.569
there is a whole world, tucked in the bottom
right of a screen somewhere, hiding in plain

00:07:45.569 --> 00:07:49.749
view.
That’s how movies build worlds. With obsessed

00:07:49.749 --> 00:07:56.669
over details that make it easier to produce
and see emotion, because there aren’t any

00:07:56.669 --> 00:08:02.139
distractions. Because the world feels real.
To see it, you have to take the time to

00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:10.619
search for it.
“This picture is of when I worked on Star

00:08:10.619 --> 00:08:17.840
Trek Nemesis. And that’s the little ship,
the plaques they have on the bridge, and it

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.599
has a lot of names of people who worked on
the movie, so I’m in there.”

